 have the purified b-cells  we were able to load on beacon and  export the b-cells that have the desired  properties  and then initiate the sequencing within  two to four weeks total  and comparing that to hybridoma  technology  the duration is quite a bit different  the process involves fusion  followed by primary and secondary  screenings  in this case we did elisa binding as  well as blocking assays  we did look at a human and  sino-cross-reactive binding during the  primary and secondary screening stages  we had to select 40 to 50 high priority  clones for  production and purification  of course we got the characterization  information but at that point we are  actually using  all of the characterization data to  select top candidates  before doing the sequencing  and by not having that sequencing  information  it can actually create challenges later  in the event that top candidates are  not as diverse in terms of sequence  as perhaps you would have liked it  if we would have known that the the  sequences  earlier  this slide shows the format of the  elizabeth blocking assay  that was used for hybridoma supernatant  screening  and so in a classical format of a  blocking assay we coated with human ptl1  ecdfc  and came in with anti-pdl1 antibody  from supernatant and detected that  either in the  presence  or  absence of biotinylated pd1  now if there is a complex formation with  biogenerated pd1  that would indicate  that it was a non-blocking antibody  and if the signal completely excluded  the by generated pt one that would  suggest that it was a blocking anybody  so this was an elisa based  quantitative evaluation of blocking  activity  and  we cast a broad net anywhere between  thirty to hundred percent in a battery  uh activity  was how we selected our heads to move  forward to uh sub cloning process during  the  primary screening of hyperdoma  supernatants  so in contrast on beacon platform we  used image based method for selecting  our blockers versus non-blockers  so on left you can see we loaded the  pens with chokey one  cells over expressing human pdl one  protein  along with single plasma b cell and  allowed to secrete antibody  and then import beads that  were coated with human pdl one  so in this format you can look at in  channel beat base binding  as well as in pen  cell-based binding  for the blocking activity we imported a  detection antibody  here uh conjugated to  alexel of flora4568  so that we could detect the presence of  anti-pdl1 antibody bound to the cells  or beads  then introduced soluble lican  pd1  fluorescently labeled here shown as  alexa floral 488  so if we see the signal in both channels  suggest that the secreted antibody is  specific to the human pdl1  but does not out compete soluble like  and pd1  but in the image above  you can see  an example of blocking antibody  where  you see a signal correlating to the  antibody  binding to human pdl1  but there is limited signal in  fitzy channel suggesting that antibody  prevents the  binding of pd1  to pdl1  either on cell  or beat  so this is the comparison of b-cell  cloning versus hyperdoma output  so on top there is the b-cell cloning  results  for either self-paced  or bead-based binders  in total we evaluated around 33 000  single plasma b-cells on the chips  over 200 of them  bound human pdl-1 on choking one cells  and of those 35 of them had  blocking activity and over 500 pound  human ptl1 on beats as well  so on bottom here you can see the  results of primary and secondary  screenings of hyperdoma supernatants  where you can see over 50  bound human pdl1 on cho k1 cells  and similarly on  sinopedial one on choke one cells as  well of those 48 had 48 had  variable degree of blocking activity  so here we identified more number of  blockers from  hybridoma approach  compared to a b-cell cloning approach  however  false positive  is a definite concern  during a hybridoma screening  the results of the blocking  assay is been shown on th